## Move semantics in C++

Since C++11 the language gives you a way to *transfer* the resources that an object owns, instead of *copying* them.  
That is what “move semantics” is all about.

---

### Why move semantics matter

* **Performance** – Copying a large object (e.g. a `std::string` with 1 MB of data, or a `std::vector` with thousands of elements) means allocating new memory and copying every byte.  
  Moving just “steals” the existing memory pointer and leaves the source in a valid but empty state.  
* **Safety** – After a move the source object is still a fully‑valid C++ object; it just doesn’t own the data anymore.  
* **Standard library compliance** – Many standard containers (e.g. `std::vector`, `std::unordered_map`) use move operations internally to keep algorithms fast.

---

### The idea

```text
lvalue  ──► copy
rvalue  ──► move
```

* **Copy** – the data is duplicated; the source keeps its original data.  
* **Move** – the data pointer is *stealed*; the source is set to a safe “empty” state (often a `nullptr` or `0`).

In C++ an *rvalue* is an expression that denotes a temporary object (or an object that can be destroyed).  
`std::move(x)` simply casts `x` to an rvalue reference `T&&`, telling the compiler: “you may move from `x`”.

---

## A minimal example

We'll write a tiny `String` class that owns a dynamic C‑style string.  
We'll give it

* a constructor (allocation)
* a destructor (deallocation)
* **copy constructor** & **copy assignment** (deep copy)
* **move constructor** & **move assignment** (resource steal)

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <cstring>   // std::strlen, std::memcpy
#include <utility>   // std::move

class String {
    char* data_;          // owned pointer
    std::size_t size_;    // length of string (excluding terminating NUL)

public:
    /* ---------- constructors / destructor ---------- */

    // default ctor
    String() : data_(nullptr), size_(0) {}

    // ctor from C-string
    explicit String(const char* s) {
        size_ = std::strlen(s);
        data_ = new char[size_ + 1];
        std::memcpy(data_, s, size_ + 1);   // copy including '\0'
    }

    // copy ctor
    String(const String& other) : data_(nullptr), size_(0) {
        if (other.size_ > 0) {
            data_ = new char[other.size_ + 1];
            std::memcpy(data_, other.data_, other.size_ + 1);
            size_ = other.size_;
        }
        std::cout << "Copy ctor\n";
    }

    // move ctor
    String(String&& other) noexcept : data_(other.data_), size_(other.size_) {
        // "steal" the resource
        other.data_ = nullptr;
        other.size_ = 0;
        std::cout << "Move ctor\n";
    }

    // copy assignment
    String& operator=(const String& other) {
        if (this != &other) {
            // free existing
            delete[] data_;

            if (other.size_ > 0) {
                data_ = new char[other.size_ + 1];
                std::memcpy(data_, other.data_, other.size_ + 1);
                size_ = other.size_;
            } else {
                data_ = nullptr;
                size_ = 0;
            }
        }
        std::cout << "Copy assign\n";
        return *this;
    }

    // move assignment
    String& operator=(String&& other) noexcept {
        if (this != &other) {
            delete[] data_;                // free current
            data_ = other.data_;           // steal
            size_ = other.size_;

            other.data_ = nullptr;         // leave source empty
            other.size_ = 0;
        }
        std::cout << "Move assign\n";
        return *this;
    }

    /* ---------- helpers ---------- */
    const char* c_str() const { return data_ ? data_ : ""; }

    /* ---------- destructor ---------- */
    ~String() { delete[] data_; }
};
```

### What happens in practice?

```cpp
String foo(const String& s) {            // returns by value
    return s;                            // copy
}

String bar(String&& s) {                 // parameter is an rvalue ref
    return std::move(s);                 // move
}

int main() {
    String hello("Hello, world!");        // ctor

    String a = hello;                    // copy ctor
    String b = std::move(hello);         // move ctor – hello is now empty

    std::cout << "hello: \"" << hello.c_str() << "\"\n"; // prints empty

    // Returning from a function
    String c = foo(a);                   // copy ctor
    String d = bar(std::move(b));        // move ctor
}
```

**Output**

```
Copy ctor
Move ctor
hello: ""
Copy ctor
Move ctor
```

Notice how moving `hello` into `b` did *not* copy the 13‑byte buffer. The move constructor just swapped pointers and set `hello` to an empty state, which is O(1). Copying would have taken O(n) time.

---

## Rules of Five / Three

If you define either a destructor, copy constructor, or copy assignment, the compiler will **not** automatically generate the move versions.  
Typical idiom:

```cpp
class MyClass {
public:
    MyClass(const MyClass&);      // copy ctor
    MyClass& operator=(const MyClass&);  // copy assign
    MyClass(MyClass&&);           // move ctor
    MyClass& operator=(MyClass&&); // move assign
    ~MyClass();                   // destructor
};
```

You can omit any that are unnecessary.  
If you don't need copying at all, delete them:

```cpp
String(const String&) = delete;
String& operator=(const String&) = delete;
```

---

## Key take‑aways

| Feature | What it does | When it happens |
|---------|--------------|-----------------|
| `T&&`  | rvalue reference | When you bind to a temporary or explicitly `std::move(x)` |
| `std::move(x)` | Casts `x` to `T&&` | Lets you *move* from `x` |
| Move constructor | Steals resources | When an object is *constructed* from an rvalue |
| Move assignment | Steals resources | When an object is *assigned* from an rvalue |
| `noexcept` on move ops | Enables optimizations | Required for `std::vector` to move elements safely |
| Rule of Five | Keep all special member functions consistent | Avoid memory leaks or double‑free |

---

### Quick cheat sheet

```cpp
// Constructor
String(const char* s);

// Move ctor
String(String&& other) noexcept
    : data_(other.data_), size_(other.size_) {
    other.data_ = nullptr;
    other.size_ = 0;
}

// Move assign
String& operator=(String&& other) noexcept {
    if (this != &other) {
        delete[] data_;
        data_ = other.data_;
        size_ = other.size_;
        other.data_ = nullptr;
        other.size_ = 0;
    }
    return *this;
}
```

That’s the essence of move semantics—transfer the “ownership” of resources instead of copying them, leading to faster and more efficient C++ code.
